82:To investigate the categorical relationships between schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders, between-group similarity and difference in the extents of GFA deviation, alongside idiosyncrasy, were evaluated. Strati /uniFB01 ed analyses of /uniFB01 rst-episode schizophrenia (FES), subchronic, and chronic schizophrenia were implemented to specify whether people in the early course of schizophrenia present WM alterations more similar to neurodevelopmental disorders than patients with later-stage schizophrenia. Betweengroup differences (e.g., schizophrenia vs. ASD, schizophrenia vs. ADHD, etc.) of deviation and idiosyncrasy based on GFA Z -scores were evaluated with a twosample t -test and F -test (schizophrenia ' s WM variances divided by neurodevelopmental ' s WM variances), respectively. Although the normative modeling has taken the age and sex effects into account when yielding WM deviation pro /uniFB01 les, we performed the propensity score weights matching for age [55] between schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders as the main results. We repeated this categorical analysis based on the entire sample of neurodevelopmental disorders to complement the main /uniFB01 ndings.
118:The categorical analysis based on the metrics derived from the diffusion tensor model [58] (axial diffusivity [SFig. 8], radial diffusivity [SFig. 9], and mean diffusivity [SFig. 10]) also largely replicated the patterns observed based on the GFA metrics.
132:As the /uniFB01 rst study to investigate the similarity and difference between schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders based on the whole-brain WM tracts deviation to the age norm, we found, generally, the similarity of deviance and idiosyncrasy of WM tracts between early-stage schizophrenia and ADHD or ASD, respectively, reduced as the disease progressed to subchronic or chronic schizophrenia. Using the WM deviation patterns as features, schizophrenia cannot be separated from neurodevelopmental disorders in the unsupervised machine learning algorithm. Moreover, schizophrenia, ADHD, and ASD shared linked cognitive dimensions driven by WM deviations. Together, our results provide novel evidence to support the neurodevelopmental model, whereby schizophrenia lies on a neurodevelopmental continuum [2].
144:Our /uniFB01 ndings provide evidence to support a neurodevelopmental model in earlier stages of the schizophrenic brain. This ' neurodevelopmental-ness ' might be driven by some shared common genetic risk among these disorders [2, 10, 11]. An alternative interpretation could be that these similarities and signi /uniFB01 cant correlations in deviation/idiosyncrasy patterns may only indicate that the vulnerability of these WM tracts are shared across disorders but do not imply that schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorders shared pathophysiology behind the seeming resemblance in the brain. The /uniFB01 nding of the loss of neurodevelopmental similarity in chronic schizophrenia might re /uniFB02 ect another notion in the neurodevelopmental model [2] that the progressive deterioration of connectome pathology in schizophrenia [63] drives varying phenotypic expression. Nonetheless, the WM idiosyncrasy in /uniFB01 rst-episode and chronic schizophrenia was better dimensionally correlated with that of ASD than that of ADHD. This result may partly explain the prior inconsistent /uniFB01 ndings on cross-diagnosis comparisons when the age effect and its relationship with the norm were not considered [16]. Notably, the categorical comparisons showed that the WM microstructural composition in ASD/ADHD was less idiosyncratic than that in schizophrenia as the illness progressed (STable 6C). This dimensional vs. categorical dissonance between neurodevelopmental disorders-schizophrenia comparison might indicate that the idiosyncratic distribution of each WM tract of neurodevelopmental disorders and schizophrenia is still largely similar, but the extent is more varying in schizophrenia at the later stages, consistent with the overall picture of the current /uniFB01 ndings. Notably, neurodevelopmental disorders share several domains of social impairment and executive dysfunction with schizophrenia at later stages [1, 8, 9]. This phenotype similarity may be attributable to other neuropathology rather than WM changes alone.
148:Echoing the Cognitive Systems in the RDoC [36], our CCA models identi /uniFB01 ed a speci /uniFB01 c cognitive dimension driven by wholebrain WM deviations crossing schizophrenia and typical neurodevelopmental disorders. We identi /uniFB01 ed that several long-range tracts and association /uniFB01 bers are involved in the working memory and intellectual functioning, particularly the tracts relaying frontal input-output information (e.g., IFOF and ILF), and those implicated
154:in sensory integration (e.g., thalamic radiations) [64]. These /uniFB01 ndings support the notion that the prefrontal cortex acts as a hub underpinning higher-order cognition [69]. The result that schizophrenia has shared linked cognition-connectivity dimensions with ASD and ADHD suggests a common neurocognitive mechanism across these disorders. Our /uniFB01 nding provides macroscale connectomic evidence to endorse further that schizophrenia may be conceptualized as a neurodevelopmental condition [2], which could feature WM deviations from normative patterns [17].
158:In conclusion, using the normative modeling to preclude the caveat of group-average approaches, which may not capture an individual ' s accurate pictures [15], this study demonstrated the general similarity between schizophrenia, particularly at the earlier stages, and neurodevelopmental disorders in WM deviations from the norm and inter-individual variability in deviation patterns. Aligning with the RDoC, our CCA /uniFB01 ndings suggest that an individual ' s WM deviations may contribute to diverse arrays of cognitive function along a continuum with phenotypic expressions from typical neurodevelopmental disorders to schizophrenia. This study provides new insights into the neurodevelopmental facet of schizophrenia and its brain basis.
